 a risk with negative financial implication; mitigation is to follow Government guidelines and focus on co-generation and by-products. MD&A adds that FRP rose Rs.15 to Rs.355/quintal for SY2026 (10.25% basic recovery) and Karnataka SAP rose Rs.250/quintal, while MSP has not been revised since 2019 (industry urging revision to Rs.39/kg) - a squeeze between rising input cost and a frozen output floor price.","risk":"Government policy changes, particularly on cane FRP and administered sugar pricing"},{"note":"BRSR environmental issues row: changes in rainfall patterns, temperatures and extreme weather events can affect crop yields and quality; negative financial implication. MD&A corroborates - Maharashtra and Karnataka saw reduced yields from early sugarcane flowering due to excess rain and shorter harvest windows.","risk":"Climate change and adverse weather affecting crop yield and quality"},{"note":"BRSR: the company is situated on the banks of the Krishna River with the Hippergi barrage nearby and sufficient water is normally available, but in a drought situation there is a shortage of water. Mitigated through effective water management practices; financial implication negative.","risk":"Water availability / drought exposure at the plants"},{"note":"BRSR: sugar production requires a timely and continuous supply of good quality cane which may be affected by improper farmer engagement. Mitigated through cordial relations with farmers and an R&D cell plus field staff visiting farmers for cultivation guidance and yield enhancement.","risk":"Farmer engagement dependency for continuous cane supply"},{"note":"BRSR flags poor labour practices as a credibility and high-turnover risk with negative financial implication. MD&A adds that human labour constitutes 52% of total sugarcane cultivation cost, so any change flows directly into cost of cultivation; increased farm mechanisation is cited as the offset.","risk":"Labour practices, farm labour cost and attrition"},{"note":"MD&A: in October 2024 the Supreme Court upheld States' power to regulate industrial alcohol, which may give state governments the ability to tax it. Material because Industrial Alcohol is the single largest revenue line at 44.66% of turnover.","risk":"Regulatory/taxation risk on industrial alcohol after the Supreme Court ruling"},{"note":"MD&A: with 5 million tonne opening stock, total domestic availability of 33.00 million tonne exceeds consumption of 28.00 million tonne, and ISO expects a global surplus of 1.625 million tonnes for 2025/26. India is likely to ship only 7.5-8 lakh tonnes in the 2025-26 season, well short of the quota, as weak global price parity discourages outbound sales; international raw sugar fell to $313/MT in Feb 2026 from $445/MT a year earlier. The company itself did not export any sugar during the year and does not export directly.","risk":"Sugar oversupply and weak export parity"}],"rpt":{},"rpt_date":"","s":"UGARSUGAR","sector":"Fast Moving Consumer Goods","sk":null,"strategies":"Operate an integrated sugar-ethanol-cogeneration model across the two units at Ugar and Jewargi, with surplus power exported to the exchange through PTC India Ltd.|Maintain a proper and adequate system of internal control so that all assets are safeguarded and protected, with the Internal Auditor reporting on almost all areas of operations.|The Board has recommended a 10% dividend (Rs. 0.10 per share) for the year, against nil in the previous year, and no amount has been transferred to Reserve for FY 2025-26. Distributions are governed by a formal Dividend Distribution Policy formulated by …","strengths":"Integrated sugar-to-alcohol-to-power model: cane crushed at two units feeds a distillery producing industrial and potable alcohol while bagasse feeds cogeneration, diversifying away from sugar alone|Multiple independent offtake channels - sugar agents and institutional buyers, oil marketing companies for ethanol, KSBCL for potable alcohol, and the power exchange 